problem:
Expand and simplify completely: \begin{align*}
x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*}

solution:
Begin by distributing in the innermost parentheses: \begin{align*}
&\ \ \ \ x\left(x(1+x)+2x\right)-3(x^2-x+2) \\&= x(x+x^2+2x) - 3(x^2-x+2)
\end{align*} Now, distribute again: \begin{align*}
x^2+x^3+2x^2-3x^2+3x-6
\end{align*} Finally, combine like terms to get \begin{align*}
\boxed{x^3+3x-6}
\end{align*}

The probability of the number appearing 0, 1, and 2 times is \begin{align*}
&P(0) = \frac{3}{4}\cdot \frac{3}{4} = \frac{9}{16},\\
&P(1) = 2\cdot\frac{1}{4}\cdot \frac{3}{4} = \frac{6}{16},
\quad\text{and}\\
&P(2) = \frac{1}{4}\cdot \frac{1}{4} = \frac{1}{16},
\end{align*} respectively. So the expected return, in dollars, to the player is \begin{align*}
P(0)\cdot (-1) + P(1)\cdot (1) + P(2)\cdot (2) &= \frac{-9 + 6 +
2}{16} \\
&= \boxed{-\frac{1}{16}}.
\end{align*}

The common ratio between consecutive terms is $\frac{3}{5}$ (you can choose any two consecutive terms and divide the second one by the first to find the common ratio). So the $n^\text{th}$ term of the sequence is $\frac{125}{9} \cdot \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^{n-1}$. Plugging in $n=8$, we get $$
\frac{125}{9} \cdot \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^{7} = \frac{5^3}{3^2} \cdot \frac{3^7}{5^7}
= \frac{3^5}{5^4}
= \boxed{\frac{243}{625}}.
$$

Geometrically, $|z + 5 - 3i|$ is the distance between the complex numbers $z$ and $-5 + 3i$ in the complex plane, and $|z - 7 + 2i|$ is the distance between $z$ and $7 - 2i.$

