problem:
Let \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
ax+3 & \text{ if }x>0, \\
ab & \text{ if }x=0, \\
bx+c & \text{ if }x<0.
\end{array}
\right.\]If $f(2)=5$, $f(0)=5$, and $f(-2)=-10$, and $a$, $b$, and $c$ are nonnegative integers, then what is $a+b+c$?

solution:
We can expand the determinant as follows:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 2 & 0 & -1 \\ 7 & 4 & -3 \\ 2 & 2 & 5 \end{vmatrix} &= 2 \begin{vmatrix} 4 & -3 \\ 2 & 5 \end{vmatrix} + (-1) \begin{vmatrix} 7 & 4 \\ 2 & 2 \end{vmatrix} \\
&= 2((4)(5) - (-3)(2)) - ((7)(2) - (4)(2)) \\
&= \boxed{46}.
\end{align*}

We will locate the numbers on the number line.  $4.5$ goes half way between $4$ and $5$ because $0.5=\frac{5}{10}=\frac{1}{2}.$.

Note that $4.45$ goes half way between $4.4$ and $4.5$ because $$0.4=\frac{4}{10}=\frac{8}{20}, \ \ 0.5=\frac{5}{10}=\frac{10}{20}, \ \ \text{and} \ \ 0.45=\frac{9}{20}.$$Likewise, $0.45$ goes half way between $0.4$ and $0.5$ because $$0.4=\frac{4}{10}=\frac{8}{20}, \ \ 0.5=\frac{5}{10}=\frac{10}{20}, \ \ \text{and} \ \ 0.45=\frac{9}{20}.$$Finally, $0.54$ goes just a little bit less than halfway between $0.5$ and $0.6$ because $0.54$ is just a little bit smaller than $0.55,$ which is half way between $0.5$ and $0.6.$

Thus, we can plot all four numbers on the same number line as follows: [asy]
size(8cm); defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
real eps=0.08; int k; int n=9;
draw((-0.5,0)--(5.5,0),Arrows(4.0));
for(k=0;k<=5;++k)

{

draw((k,-eps)--(k,eps));

label("$"+string(k)+"$",(k,0),2.5*S);

}
for(k=1;k<10;++k)

{

draw((k/10,-eps/2)--(k/10,eps/2));

draw((4+k/10,-eps/2)--(4+k/10,eps/2));

}
dot("4.5",(4.5,0),3*N,red);
dot("4.45",(4.45,0),3*S);
dot("0.45",(0.45,0),3*N,green);
dot("0.54",(0.54,0),3*S);
[/asy] Therefore, the sum of the smallest and the largest numbers is $4.5 + 0.45 = \boxed{4.95}.$

Construct a right triangle with legs 1 and $x.$  Let the angle opposite the side length $x$ be $\theta.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C;

A = (2,1.8);
B = (0,0);
C = (2,0);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(A,C,B,8));

label("$\theta$", B + (0.7,0.3));
label("$1$", (B + C)/2, S);
label("$x$", (A + C)/2, E);
label("$\sqrt{x^2 + 1}$", (A + B)/2, NW);
[/asy]

Then $\tan \theta = x,$ so $\theta = \arctan x.$  Then
\[\cos (\arctan x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}},\]so
\[\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + 1}} = x.\]Squaring both sides, we get
\[\frac{1}{x^2 + 1} = x^2,\]so $x^4 + x^2 - 1 = 0.$  By the quadratic formula,
\[x^2 = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}.\]Since $x^2$ is positive,
\[x^2 = \boxed{\frac{-1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}}.\]

Completing the square in $x$ and $y,$ we get
\[\left( x - \frac{1}{2} \right)^2 + \left( y - \frac{1}{2} \right)^2 = \frac{1}{2}.\]This represents the equation of the circle with center $\left( \frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2} \right)$ and radius $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}.$
[asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

draw(Circle((0,0),1));
draw((0,0)--(1,0));

label("$\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$", (1/2,0), S);

dot("$(\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2})$", (0,0), N);
dot((1,0));
[/asy]
Hence, the largest possible value of $x$ is $\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} = \boxed{\frac{1 + \sqrt{2}}{2}}.$

