solution:
We may carry out long division in base 4 just as in base 10. We have  \[
\begin{array}{cc|cccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 3 & 3 \\
\cline{3-6}
1 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && 3&3& \downarrow \\ \cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && 0&3&3 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && & 3&3 \\ \cline{5-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} && & & 0 \\
\end{array}
\]for a quotient of $\boxed{33_4}$. Note that in the above calculation we have used that $11_4\cdot3_4=5_{10}\cdot3_{10}=15_{10}=33_4$. Then, for the second step we used $33_4$ divided by $11_4$ is $3_4$.

First, we use the distributive property to expand the first two factors:

\begin{align*}
6(x+2)(x+3) &= (6\cdot x + 6 \cdot 2) (x+3)\\
&=(6x+12)(x+3)
\end{align*}We use the distributive property again by adding the product of $6x+12$ and $x$ to the product of $6x+12$ and 3:

\begin{align*}
(6x+12)(x+3) &= (6x+12) \cdot x +(6x+12) \cdot 3\\
&= x(6x+12) + 3(6x+12)
\end{align*}We use the distributive property again and combine like terms:

\begin{align*}
x(6x+12) + 3(6x+12) &= 6x^2 + 12x + 18x+ 36\\
&= \boxed{6x^2 + 30x + 36}
\end{align*}

